1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,384
It's the end of a debugging session. One
file holds three edits: a tuning change you

2
00:00:09,424 --> 00:00:13,780
were trying, the bug fix you found, and
a debug print you forgot to remove.

3
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:18,703
The fix has to go out now, on its own, so
that it can be reviewed and, if necessary,

4
00:00:18,743 --> 00:00:19,875
reverted on its own.

5
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:24,855
You can do that. Git lets you stage one of
the three edits, which means marking it

6
00:00:24,895 --> 00:00:29,535
for the next commit, and commit it. And
the commit you make will contain a version

7
00:00:29,575 --> 00:00:33,986
of the file that has never existed on
your disk, and that nobody has ever run.

8
00:00:36,326 --> 00:00:40,774
That's the trade at the centre of this
video. Partial staging gives you clean

9
00:00:40,814 --> 00:00:44,505
commits from a messy working tree,
the folder of files you edit.

10
00:00:44,545 --> 00:00:47,944
The price is a snapshot in the
index that was never tested.

11
00:00:47,984 --> 00:00:51,675
You'll learn the technique, and
the check that removes the risk.

12
00:00:51,715 --> 00:00:55,172
And that file nobody has ever
run? You'll meet it by its ID.

13
00:00:57,012 --> 00:01:00,772
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:00,812 --> 00:01:05,817
Three topics today, all consequences of
the last video's one idea: the index is a

15
00:01:05,857 --> 00:01:09,430
full snapshot that sits between
HEAD and the working tree.

16
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:14,414
The index is Git's proposal for the next
commit, and HEAD is the last commit you

17
00:01:14,454 --> 00:01:14,725
made.

18
00:01:15,065 --> 00:01:20,428
First: three places for a version of a file
give three pairwise comparisons, and git

19
00:01:20,468 --> 00:01:24,093
diff performs a different one
depending on its arguments.

20
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:29,367
Second: git add -p, which stages part of
a file. Third: git add -N, intent to add,

21
00:01:29,407 --> 00:01:34,512
which makes a new file visible to the
commands that would otherwise overlook it.

22
00:01:36,352 --> 00:01:37,931
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:38,271 --> 00:01:39,683
After this video you can:

24
00:01:40,023 --> 00:01:45,507
Say which two trees each of git diff, git
diff --cached and git diff HEAD compares.

25
00:01:45,847 --> 00:01:50,845
Stage part of a file with git add -p,
including a hunk that cannot be split.

26
00:01:51,185 --> 00:01:55,090
Explain the risk of a partially staged
commit, and the check that removes it.

27
00:01:55,430 --> 00:01:59,346
Make an untracked file visible
to git diff with git add -N.

28
00:02:01,186 --> 00:02:01,807
Three diffs.

29
00:02:02,147 --> 00:02:06,928
git diff compares the index with the working
tree. It answers: what could I still

30
00:02:06,968 --> 00:02:07,286
stage?

31
00:02:07,626 --> 00:02:11,752
git diff --cached, synonym --staged,
compares HEAD with the index.

32
00:02:11,792 --> 00:02:14,593
It answers: what will
the next commit change?

33
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:19,718
git diff HEAD compares HEAD with the working
tree. It answers: what changed since

34
00:02:19,758 --> 00:02:21,565
the last commit, staged or not?

35
00:02:21,905 --> 00:02:26,839
Quick quiz, with the picture. You've
edited the file and not staged it.

36
00:02:26,879 --> 00:02:31,533
Which diff is empty: git diff, git
diff --cached, or git diff HEAD?

37
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:32,654
Say your answer.

38
00:02:37,494 --> 00:02:43,314
git diff --cached. It compares HEAD with
the index, and those two boxes still agree.

39
00:02:43,654 --> 00:02:48,530
Now you stage the edit. This time the empty
one is git diff, because the index and

40
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:50,268
the working tree agree again.

41
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:55,576
Two of them can cancel out. If the working
tree equals HEAD and the index differs

42
00:02:55,616 --> 00:03:00,583
from both, git diff HEAD is empty, while
each of the other two shows a change, in

43
00:03:00,623 --> 00:03:05,775
opposite directions. So an empty git diff
HEAD proves nothing about the next commit.

44
00:03:05,815 --> 00:03:09,422
And none of the three shows a
file that has no index entry.

45
00:03:09,762 --> 00:03:13,980
The rule for practice: make git diff
--cached the last command before git commit.

46
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:16,450
It's the review of what
you're about to record.

47
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:21,736
Partial staging. In one sentence: git add
-p walks through the difference between

48
00:03:21,776 --> 00:03:26,661
the index and the working tree one hunk
at a time, and stages only the hunks you

49
00:03:26,701 --> 00:03:31,647
accept, so one edited file can feed several
commits and leave a remainder that is

50
00:03:31,687 --> 00:03:33,493
never committed. It is 🟢 SAFE.

51
00:03:33,834 --> 00:03:37,678
A hunk is a block of changed lines
with its context, as in git diff.

52
00:03:37,718 --> 00:03:40,820
Git asks about each hunk, and
you answer with a letter.

53
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:44,631
y and n: stage this hunk, or
leave it. s: split the hunk.

54
00:03:44,671 --> 00:03:48,696
It's offered only when unchanged
lines separate the changes in it.

55
00:03:48,736 --> 00:03:52,514
e: open the hunk in your editor,
and stage the edited version.

56
00:03:52,554 --> 00:03:56,703
a and d: stage, or leave, this hunk
and all later hunks of the file.

57
00:03:56,743 --> 00:04:01,507
q: stop. Hunks already accepted are
staged. The remaining letters move between

58
00:04:01,547 --> 00:04:04,956
hunks, search, print the hunk
again, and print the help.

59
00:04:05,296 --> 00:04:11,260
The same selector runs in the other
direction in git restore -p, git restore

60
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,551
--staged -p and git reset -p.

61
00:04:13,891 --> 00:04:17,356
Inside .git. One new blob
and one changed index entry.

62
00:04:17,396 --> 00:04:20,667
A blob is the object that
holds one file's content.

63
00:04:20,707 --> 00:04:25,730
This one is HEAD's version plus the hunks
you accepted: content that has never

64
00:04:25,770 --> 00:04:26,898
existed as a file.

65
00:04:27,238 --> 00:04:32,436
The risk, and the check. After a partial
add, three versions of the file exist.

66
00:04:32,476 --> 00:04:37,939
HEAD has none of your edits. The working
tree has all of them, and is what you ran.

67
00:04:37,979 --> 00:04:43,244
The index has the accepted hunks alone,
and that version has never run anywhere.

68
00:04:43,284 --> 00:04:48,415
Before you commit it, copy the staged
snapshot out of the index and test that.

69
00:04:48,455 --> 00:04:53,852
git checkout-index --all --prefix=<dir>/
🟢 SAFE writes every index entry as a file

70
00:04:53,892 --> 00:04:57,632
below the directory you name.
The trailing slash matters.

71
00:04:57,972 --> 00:05:02,933
Editing a hunk. When changed lines touch
each other, they form one hunk, and s is

72
00:05:02,973 --> 00:05:07,872
not offered. The answer e opens the hunk
as a patch, a text of added and removed

73
00:05:07,912 --> 00:05:11,699
lines. Deleting a + line keeps
that addition out of the index.

74
00:05:12,039 --> 00:05:16,052
Replacing the - of a removed line
with a space keeps that removal out.

75
00:05:16,092 --> 00:05:20,047
Git then recounts the hunk header
and applies the patch to the index.

76
00:05:20,087 --> 00:05:24,911
The textbook's section on dangerous edge
cases has a warning in its heading that you

77
00:05:24,951 --> 00:05:28,790
should remember: a hunk edit can
stage content that exists nowhere.

78
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:34,181
Intent to add. In one sentence: git add
-N <path> gives a new file an index entry

79
00:05:34,221 --> 00:05:39,084
without staging its content, so that
commands which compare the index with the

80
00:05:39,124 --> 00:05:42,415
working tree stop overlooking
the file. It is 🟢 SAFE.

81
00:05:42,455 --> 00:05:46,689
The manual: "Record only the fact
that the path will be added later.

82
00:05:46,729 --> 00:05:50,586
An entry for the path is placed
in the index with no content".

83
00:05:50,926 --> 00:05:56,095
Why is it needed? A new file is invisible
to git diff, because there's no entry to

84
00:05:56,135 --> 00:06:01,368
compare it with. The hunk selector reads
the same comparison as git diff, so it has

85
00:06:01,408 --> 00:06:03,845
the same blind spot, and the same cure.

86
00:06:04,185 --> 00:06:07,644
Inside .git, the entry carries
an intent-to-add flag.

87
00:06:07,684 --> 00:06:12,860
Index format version 2 has no room for
it, so Git writes the index as version 3

88
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:17,481
while such an entry exists, and as
version 2 again after the real add.

89
00:06:17,821 --> 00:06:23,013
When not to. Partial staging is the wrong
tool when you can't test the result and

90
00:06:23,053 --> 00:06:27,793
have no CI behind you, no server that
tests every commit: the commit is by

91
00:06:27,833 --> 00:06:33,024
construction untested. And e is the wrong
tool when s would do, because an edited

92
00:06:33,064 --> 00:06:38,256
hunk is the one place where you can stage
lines that are in neither HEAD nor your

93
00:06:38,296 --> 00:06:38,579
file.

94
00:06:40,419 --> 00:06:45,192
The textbook's analogy for git add -p:
marking, on a manuscript full of your own

95
00:06:45,232 --> 00:06:50,245
edits, the blocks that go to the printer
today. It breaks where it matters most: the

96
00:06:50,285 --> 00:06:55,058
printer receives a page that never lay on
your desk in that form, and nobody has

97
00:06:55,098 --> 00:06:56,622
read that page as a whole.

98
00:06:56,962 --> 00:07:01,997
For the three diffs, picture a triangle.
Three corners: HEAD, index, working tree.

99
00:07:02,037 --> 00:07:07,011
Three sides: three diffs. If you know two
sides, you don't automatically know the

100
00:07:07,051 --> 00:07:09,363
third, because two changes can cancel.

101
00:07:11,203 --> 00:07:14,165
Now the same three
corners, with real values.

102
00:07:14,505 --> 00:07:17,964
Three boxes with the blob ID
of each version above it.

103
00:07:18,004 --> 00:07:22,565
TOP_K was changed and staged, so it
differs between HEAD and the index.

104
00:07:22,605 --> 00:07:27,555
MIN_SCORE was changed and not staged,
so it differs between the index and the

105
00:07:27,595 --> 00:07:31,832
working tree. The short arrows are
git diff --cached and git diff.

106
00:07:31,872 --> 00:07:35,785
The long arrow underneath is git
diff HEAD, which spans both.

107
00:07:40,125 --> 00:07:45,563
The state: MM. Before each of the next three
snippets, say which lines the diff will

108
00:07:45,603 --> 00:07:48,758
show. Start with plain git
diff. Say it out loud.

109
00:08:05,798 --> 00:08:14,106
Read the index line of each diff:
399da0e..5853020, then bd144df..399da0e, then

110
00:08:14,146 --> 00:08:21,925
bd144df..5853020. bd144df is the blob
in HEAD. 399da0e is the staged blob.

111
00:08:21,965 --> 00:08:28,053
5853020 is the ID that the
working-tree content would get.

112
00:08:28,093 --> 00:08:33,653
No object with that ID exists
until you add the file.

113
00:08:33,693 --> 00:08:37,774
Each command compares two of the three.

114
00:08:38,114 --> 00:08:42,227
Now the script puts HEAD's content
back into the working tree only.

115
00:08:42,267 --> 00:08:45,512
Predict the output of git
diff HEAD. Say it out loud.

116
00:08:45,552 --> 00:08:46,132
I'll wait.

117
00:08:50,972 --> 00:08:55,403
Nothing. git diff HEAD is empty,
because the files on disk equal HEAD.

118
00:08:55,443 --> 00:09:00,705
The other two comparisons each show one
changed line, in opposite directions, and a

119
00:09:00,745 --> 00:09:03,069
commit made now would set TOP_K to 8.

120
00:09:03,409 --> 00:09:07,973
The file from the hook, with its
three edits. A tuning change, TOP_K.

121
00:09:08,013 --> 00:09:11,843
A bug fix, the guard for a zero
vector. And a debug print.

122
00:09:23,183 --> 00:09:28,171
Answering ? prints what each letter does,
and q leaves without staging anything.

123
00:09:28,211 --> 00:09:31,879
Now the real pass. The fix must
become a commit of its own.

124
00:09:31,919 --> 00:09:36,970
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds: write
the letters you would answer, hunk by

125
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:39,358
hunk. Pause me, and write your answer.

126
00:09:50,198 --> 00:09:55,200
Git first offered two hunks, because the
TOP_K line and the guard were close enough

127
00:09:55,240 --> 00:10:00,241
to share context. s split the first one:
the counter went from one of two to one of

128
00:10:00,281 --> 00:10:04,857
three. The answers were no, yes, no. If
you forgot the split, that's normal.

129
00:10:04,897 --> 00:10:07,043
Almost everyone does the first time.

130
00:10:07,383 --> 00:10:12,993
MM, and git diff --cached shows the
guard alone. The staged blob is a7cba42.

131
00:10:13,033 --> 00:10:16,934
HEAD's blob is 177cb37, with
none of the three edits.

132
00:10:16,974 --> 00:10:22,807
The working tree would be e548d1b, with
all three. The middle one has never run

133
00:10:22,847 --> 00:10:27,639
anywhere. That's the file from the
hook, and now you know its ID.

134
00:10:27,979 --> 00:10:32,311
This is the point of the whole
segment. git checkout-index --all

135
00:10:32,351 --> 00:10:37,297
--prefix=../staged-snapshot/ 🟢 SAFE
exports the staged snapshot as files.

136
00:10:37,337 --> 00:10:41,874
The diff confirms that the export
lacks the two lines you left out.

137
00:10:41,914 --> 00:10:47,407
Run your tests in that directory. Then
you've tested what you're about to commit,

138
00:10:47,447 --> 00:10:50,140
and not what happens to be on your disk.

139
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:58,141
Commit the fix, then a second pass:
yes to TOP_K, no to the debug print.

140
00:10:58,181 --> 00:11:03,039
Two commits, each doing one thing, and
a remainder that is never committed.

141
00:11:08,879 --> 00:11:13,614
Here the changed lines touch each other,
so the prompt offers no s, and typing it

142
00:11:13,654 --> 00:11:14,793
anyway doesn't help.

143
00:11:21,133 --> 00:11:25,980
The answer e opens the hunk as a patch,
with Git's own instructions below it.

144
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:29,027
In the editor, the line
+debug: true is deleted.

145
00:11:32,367 --> 00:11:36,111
Staged: the two settings.
Not staged: the debug line.

146
00:11:36,451 --> 00:11:40,842
A new file exists. Predict
what git diff shows for it.

147
00:11:40,882 --> 00:11:42,565
Make your prediction.

148
00:11:42,905 --> 00:11:45,683
Nothing: there's no index
entry to compare it with.

149
00:11:46,023 --> 00:11:51,619
After git add -N 🟢 SAFE, status lists the
path as a new file under "Changes not

150
00:11:51,659 --> 00:11:56,899
staged for commit", and its entry holds
e69de29, the ID of the empty blob.

151
00:11:57,239 --> 00:12:02,438
git diff shows the whole file as an addition,
git diff --cached shows nothing, and

152
00:12:02,478 --> 00:12:04,866
git commit refuses: nothing is staged.

153
00:12:05,206 --> 00:12:09,957
And the index version: 3 while the
intent-to-add entry exists, 2 again after the

154
00:12:09,997 --> 00:12:10,496
real add.

155
00:12:12,336 --> 00:12:13,984
Five mistakes to watch for.

156
00:12:14,324 --> 00:12:18,380
Reading an empty git diff HEAD as
"the next commit changes nothing".

157
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:23,380
Root cause: two differences cancelled; the
working tree equals HEAD while the index

158
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:24,464
differs from both.

159
00:12:24,804 --> 00:12:27,970
Committing a partially staged
file without testing it.

160
00:12:28,010 --> 00:12:32,779
Root cause: the tests ran against the
working tree, and the index holds a version

161
00:12:32,819 --> 00:12:34,501
that never existed as a file.

162
00:12:34,841 --> 00:12:39,889
Looking for s on a hunk that cannot be split.
Root cause: split is offered only when

163
00:12:39,929 --> 00:12:44,552
unchanged lines separate the changes;
touching lines are one hunk and need e.

164
00:12:44,892 --> 00:12:49,673
A new file is missing from the commit
although the diff "looked complete".

165
00:12:49,713 --> 00:12:54,950
Root cause: an untracked file has no index
entry, so no diff shows it; git add -N

166
00:12:54,990 --> 00:12:56,187
when you create it.

167
00:12:56,527 --> 00:13:00,180
Trusting an edited hunk without
reading git diff --cached.

168
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:05,400
Root cause: a hunk edit can stage content
that exists nowhere, neither in HEAD nor

169
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:06,164
in the file.

170
00:13:08,004 --> 00:13:13,350
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's classic
"works on my machine" commit lacks a new

171
00:13:13,390 --> 00:13:16,940
file. The code runs locally
because the file is on disk.

172
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:22,005
git diff looked complete. git commit -a
skipped the file, for a reason the next

173
00:13:22,045 --> 00:13:25,275
video explains. And CI fails
with a missing module.

174
00:13:25,315 --> 00:13:30,596
git add -N when you create a file puts it
into every later git diff, git add -p and

175
00:13:30,636 --> 00:13:31,493
git commit -a.

176
00:13:31,833 --> 00:13:36,922
And the reason partial staging is worth its
price: a commit that does one thing can

177
00:13:36,962 --> 00:13:40,691
be reviewed, reverted, cherry-picked
and bisected on its own.

178
00:13:40,731 --> 00:13:45,572
Later parts of the course teach each of
those. Debugging sessions don't produce

179
00:13:45,612 --> 00:13:50,330
changes in that shape. Partial staging
sorts one messy working tree into such

180
00:13:50,370 --> 00:13:54,222
commits. CI is the backstop, and
the export is the local check.

181
00:13:56,062 --> 00:13:59,765
Your turn. Do Lab 2.2,
"Partial staging", in

182
00:13:59,805 --> 00:14:03,337
lab-manual/m02-working-tree-index-head.md.

183
00:14:03,677 --> 00:14:08,938
Before each pass of git add -p, write down
the answers you intend to give, hunk by

184
00:14:08,978 --> 00:14:14,240
hunk. Before each commit, predict the output
of git diff --cached and of git diff,

185
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:15,210
and check both.

186
00:14:17,050 --> 00:14:22,206
Q51: "You staged half of a file with git
add -p, using e where s was not offered.

187
00:14:22,246 --> 00:14:27,467
Why is that commit riskier than a normal
one, what can e get wrong, and how do you

188
00:14:27,507 --> 00:14:29,712
test what you are about to commit?"

189
00:14:32,052 --> 00:14:36,872
Answer out loud. A strong answer counts
the versions of the file that exist after

190
00:14:36,912 --> 00:14:41,612
the partial add and says which one was
run. It explains what an edited hunk can

191
00:14:41,652 --> 00:14:46,472
produce that a split can't. And it ends
with a concrete procedure for testing the

192
00:14:46,512 --> 00:14:49,172
staged snapshot, with
the backstop behind it.

193
00:14:51,012 --> 00:14:56,119
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: git diff compares

194
00:14:56,159 --> 00:15:01,266
the index with the working tree, git diff
--cached compares HEAD with the index, and

195
00:15:01,306 --> 00:15:05,800
git diff HEAD compares HEAD with the
working tree. Two of them can cancel.

196
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:11,323
git add -p stages chosen hunks. I split with
s when there's context between changes,

197
00:15:11,363 --> 00:15:16,236
and edit with e when there isn't. A
partially staged commit records a tree that

198
00:15:16,276 --> 00:15:21,272
never existed in my working tree, so I
export it with git checkout-index and test

199
00:15:21,312 --> 00:15:26,309
that. And git add -N gives a new file an
entry with no content, so that diffs and

200
00:15:26,349 --> 00:15:28,112
the hunk selector can see it.

201
00:15:29,952 --> 00:15:33,738
Read sections 5.4 to 5.6 of Chapter 5.

202
00:15:34,078 --> 00:15:39,953
Do Exercise 2.3, Level 1, "three
diffs, three comparisons", in

203
00:15:39,993 --> 00:15:43,102
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

204
00:15:43,442 --> 00:15:48,317
Challenge: Exercise 2.8, Level 3, "the
commit that took more than was staged", in

205
00:15:48,357 --> 00:15:49,166
the same file.

206
00:15:49,506 --> 00:15:54,392
Today you sorted one messy file into
clean commits, and you know how to test a

207
00:15:54,432 --> 00:15:59,381
snapshot that never existed on disk. Do
the partial staging lab before the next

208
00:15:59,421 --> 00:16:04,685
video. Next time: staging deletions and
